WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:04.870
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.190
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:07.470
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.710
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.530
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:19.510
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:20.650
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:25.540
Are you ready for a baby shower?

9
00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.980
I sure am.

10
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.560
It's Jan's baby shower today.

11
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:35.940
At a company she no longer works at, with a man she no longer dates and is not the father of her baby.

12
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.860
It's going to be a huge success.

13
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:38.760
Oh, yeah.

14
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.120
Yeah.

15
00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.400
All the elements are there.

16
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:40.720
Right?

17
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:41.480
Yes.

18
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.820
People she doesn't really like are throwing her a baby shower.

19
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.100
And they don't like her.

20
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.760
It's going to be great.

21
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.780
It's Season 5, Episode 4.

22
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.780
This episode was written by Erin Shore and directed by Greg Daniels.

23
00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.180
I have a summary.

24
00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.360
Let's do it.

25
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.980
Michael and the party planning committee, still headed by Phyllis, throw a baby shower for Jan.

26
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:09.800
But they are unaware that she has already given birth to her daughter Astrid.

27
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:19.240
Jan's presence in The Office causes Michael to be rude to Holly, and Dwight spends the day putting Jan's luxury stroller to the teSt.

28
00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:24.420
Meanwhile, Jim and Pam feel the effects of their long-distance relationship.

29
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.180
Oh, that was hard to watch.

30
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.240
They were out of sync.

31
00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:27.940
They were.

32
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.740
You don't like to see that.

33
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:30.780
No, I don't.

34
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.580
Fast fact number one, new writer alert.

35
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.240
Erin Shore joined the writing staff this year in Season 5.

36
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.160
Erin had been a writer on Everybody Loves Raymond, which is one of my favorite sitcoms of all time.

37
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.320
It is such a great ensemble sitcom.

38
00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.280
Do you know that one where Marie goes to art class and she makes the sculpture?

39
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:53.840
And then, guys, look it up.

40
00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:59.400
She makes a sculpture and she's so proud and she unveils it and it looks like a vagina.

41
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.420
But she doesn't know.

42
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.780
But everybody else is like, Marie, what inspired you to make this piece?

43
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:06.920
Oh, my gosh.

44
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:07.640
It's so good.

45
00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.280
Her character was so good.

46
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:16.960
So, anyway, I was very excited to have Erin join the writing staff because then I could ask him tons of questions about Everybody Loves Raymond.

47
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.360
And you did.

48
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:19.100
I did.

49
00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.840
You chatted him up a lot about it.

50
00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.660
Well, we have some news.

51
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:23.700
Angela, do you want to share?

52
00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:24.920
I do.

53
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.740
So, I reached out to Erin.

54
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.460
We had been trying to get a hold of him.

55
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.840
You slid into his DMs.

56
00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:30.140
I did.

57
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.440
He was like, I don't even know.

58
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.360
Do I have DMs?

59
00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:32.980
I'm like, yes.

60
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:37.780
But I found an old email and we got in touch and it was so fun to talk to him.

61
00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:39.580
He's a huge fan of the podcaSt.

62
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:47.420
Jenna, he told me that he listens to the podcast in the evenings and then sometimes when he drifts off to sleep, he dreams like that he's in the podcast with us.

63
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.180
Oh, my goodness.

64
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.600
Is that a good dream or a nightmare?

65
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.120
I don't know.

66
00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:52.280
I hope it's a good thing.

67
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.000
I hope so.

68
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.260
Erin had lots of great stuff to share.

69
00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:56.820
We're going to hit him up quite a bit.

70
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.240
We have Erin now.

71
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.860
We have James and also Randy Cordrick.

72
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.240
Yes.

73
00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:08.140
When Angela told me that she got a hold of Erin, I know you were going to ask him about the office, but I had an Everybody Loves Raymond question.

74
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.520
Still, this poor man.

75
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.800
I cornered him on set and now you're calling him on the phone and my question is still about Everybody Loves Raymond.

76
00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:15.880
But here's the thing.

77
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:22.620
I remember that there was this episode that was based on a story from his real life, but I couldn't remember all the details.

78
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:23.960
It's the episode.

79
00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:25.980
It's called 10th Anniversary.

80
00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.280
And Ray and Deborah are celebrating their 10th anniversary and they want to watch their wedding tape.

81
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:35.520
And they get it out and they put it in the VCR.

82
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:37.040
They press play.

83
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.680
And Ray has recorded a football game over their wedding tape.

84
00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:49.880
And I remember that there is some story where I think Erin recorded over his wedding tape with his wife.

85
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.300
That is true.

86
00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:52.420
I knew it.

87
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:53.000
That is true.

88
00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:54.340
So this is what happened.

89
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:54.700
Okay.

90
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.280
So Erin was a fan of Everybody Loves Raymond.

91
00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.960
It was a show he wanted to meet on.

92
00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:03.680
So he thought, oh, I'll record a few episodes, watch them, really study the show.

93
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:06.720
So when I go in and meet, you know, I have all this information.

94
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.040
So he popped in a VHS.

95
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.960
Guys, this dates us all a little bit.

96
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:14.320
And he hit record and it was his wedding video.

97
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:15.140
Yeah.

98
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:17.880
And back then, you guys, it's not like you have multiple copies.

99
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.440
Like you get your wedding video tape and that's it.

100
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:20.800
Yeah.

101
00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:21.620
Yeah.

102
00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.000
His wife was really upset.

103
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:23.760
Oh, no.

104
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.100
And he went in to meet on Everybody Loves Raymond and he told that story.

105
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.540
He was like, I taped over my wedding video to watch Everybody Loves Raymond.

106
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.620
So my wife is really mad.

107
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:35.920
You got to get me this job.

108
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.440
And he got the job.

109
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.320
And then that story made it into an episode.

110
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.000
Yes.

111
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.940
Oh, I love it.

112
00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.100
A lot of Erin's life ends up in scripts, you guys.

113
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.040
There's a few episodes coming up that his actual life inspired things that Michael does.

114
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.540
And I can't wait to share those with you.

115
00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:54.380
I'm so glad we're in touch with him.

116
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:54.960
I know.

117
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:56.800
Fast fact number two.

118
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.000
Angela, we shot this episode in August of 2008.

119
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:07.720
And since we brought it up before, I thought it was worth mentioning that this same month,

120
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:15.500
Steve Carell won his second surfboard at the 2018 Choice Awards for TV Comedy Actor.

121
00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.700
Steve, where did you put your surfboards?

122
00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.180
Back-to-pack surfboards.

123
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:19.580
Wow.

124
00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.880
When we have him on the podcast, is it going to be weird if we ask him where his surfboards are?

125
00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.900
It depends where you put him.

126
00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.300
It's going to be such a weird question.

127
00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:37.360
Well, also, this same year, the movie Juno won Best Comedy Movie and Rainn Wilson was in that.

128
00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:37.920
Mm-hmm.

129
00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:41.540
Also, this same year, we won a SAG Award.

130
00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:42.480
Yes.

131
00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.760
So this was like the awards and the surfboards were coming in now.

132
00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:47.260
Yeah.

133
00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:47.680
Right?

134
00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:48.720
It was really cool.

135
00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:55.500
And I remember at the 2008 SAG Awards, we were sitting at the table and everybody was like,

136
00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.340
who's going to give the speech if we win?

137
00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:59.240
And we all assumed it would be Steve.

138
00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:00.700
Like, Steve will give the speech.

139
00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.680
And Steve said, guys, this is an ensemble.

140
00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.720
I don't want to always speak for the caSt.

141
00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:06.180
That doesn't feel right to me.

142
00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.880
This is an ensemble award.

143
00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.320
I think someone else should speak.

144
00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.900
And I mean, first of all, amazing, right?

145
00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:13.820
He's so sweet.

146
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:14.220
Awesome.

147
00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.140
And so then Rainn said, I think Jenna should do it.

148
00:06:18.940 --> 00:06:23.100
And I feel like none of us really thought we were going to win because we'd won the year before.

149
00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.560
And I said, oh, okay.

150
00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:25.320
Yeah, sure.

151
00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:25.920
I'll do it.

152
00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:26.820
And then we won.

153
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.200
And I had to give a speech at an award show.

154
00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.400
And you did such a great job.

155
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.060
You were so articulate and well-spoken.

156
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:37.700
And I was in awe because we didn't think we were going to win.

157
00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:38.020
No.

158
00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:39.280
It was a total surprise.

159
00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:40.360
And then we won.

160
00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.020
And I'm like walking to the stage.

161
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.220
And I'm like, oh, my God.

162
00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.220
Well, I know you guys are tired of me talking about being pregnant.

163
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.880
But I was pregnant at that award show.

164
00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.700
And it's a very odd thing to be super huge and pregnant at an award show.

165
00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.620
But you did such a great job.

166
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:55.400
Thank you.

167
00:06:55.720 --> 00:07:01.340
I ended up kind of going down a rabbit hole of all the cool things that happened for our show in 2008.

168
00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:02.600
It was a good year.

169
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:03.680
It was a good year.

170
00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:07.380
James Carey sent me the call sheet for this episode.

171
00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:08.340
Jamesopedia.

172
00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:09.400
Jamesopedia.

173
00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:18.360
And on the call sheet, it said, there is a screening of Rainn Wilson's new movie, The Rocker, at Universal City Walk for the cast and crew.

174
00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:19.500
All are welcome.

175
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.680
So that was also happening.

176
00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:23.760
That's a lot happening.

177
00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:24.560
All right.

178
00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:26.040
Fast fact number three.

179
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.060
This episode is a baby shower.

180
00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.600
So I'm going to tell you some stuff about baby showers.

181
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.840
You did a deep dive on baby showers?

182
00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:33.240
I did.

183
00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.540
What is there to know?

184
00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:35.620
It's a baby coming.

185
00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:36.920
Get some presents.

186
00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:37.940
Get everyone ready.

187
00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.240
What else is there to know?

188
00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:44.720
Well, baby showers are believed to date all the way back to the Egyptian and Greek civilizations.

189
00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:45.740
Whoa.

190
00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.840
But baby showers as we know them today began during the baby boom era of World War II.

191
00:07:51.840 --> 00:08:01.660
So traditionally, the baby shower was thrown by a close family member or a friend, and the mother-to-be opened gifts in a decorated chair.

192
00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.000
Well, Jan has a very decorated chair.

193
00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:05.000
I noticed.

194
00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:05.680
Yeah.

195
00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:07.140
Thank you, party planning committee.

196
00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:08.540
Thank you, Phyllis.

197
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:12.320
Well, I think Angela did it because Phyllis is making her do everything.

198
00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:13.800
So thank you, Angela.

199
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:14.700
Okay.

200
00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:16.200
I don't know.

201
00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:24.880
Well, listen, nowadays we have all kinds of ways that we can celebrate a new baby, and here are some new updated baby showers.

202
00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:26.680
A diaper shower.

203
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.760
That's where all you bring are diapers in different sizes.

204
00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:36.300
It's like if you go to your baby shower, it's like you went to Costco and bought out all the diapers.

205
00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.460
You just go home with every diaper because diapers are expensive.

206
00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:40.760
What's the next one?

207
00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.260
Grandparents shower.

208
00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:51.300
This is a shower that you throw for the grandparents, and you give them stuff to keep at their house for when the baby comes over.

209
00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:53.120
Boo.

210
00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:58.140
I love you, grandparents, but come on.

211
00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.500
Grandparents, they love to go get that stuff.

212
00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:01.540
Yeah, I don't know.

213
00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:02.920
Here's another one.

214
00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:03.220
Okay.

215
00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:04.160
I've heard of these.

216
00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:04.880
A sprinkle.

217
00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:05.900
What?

218
00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.340
You don't have a baby shower.

219
00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:08.340
You have a sprinkle.

220
00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:09.480
It sounds like you're going pee.

221
00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:10.280
It does.

222
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:21.240
Because this is a shower that you throw for your second or third or fourth babies to collect maybe some of the things if you are having a baby of a different gender.

223
00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:22.060
Right, right.

224
00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.620
Or if you need to refresh.

225
00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:24.280
Oh, my God.

226
00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:25.200
I just got it.

227
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.100
A shower and a sprinkle.

228
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:27.580
Yeah.

229
00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:28.740
It's not a full shower.

230
00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:29.960
It's just a sprinkle.

231
00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.300
Every time you say it, I just think of tinkling.

232
00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:33.420
I do, too.

233
00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.520
So, all right, the final one is a sip and see.

234
00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:39.240
Does this involve wine?

235
00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:40.360
It does.

236
00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:44.920
It is a celebration planned by the new parents after the baby has arrived.

237
00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.520
Oh, you sip and you get to see the baby.

238
00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:47.940
Yes.

239
00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:53.260
So, I have to say, what we are going to see in this episode is technically a sip and see.

240
00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.180
You know what?

241
00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.800
I have been to a sip and see, but I didn't even know it.

242
00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:58.820
I love the name sip and see.

243
00:09:59.060 --> 00:09:59.700
Sip and see.

244
00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.800
Can I have a sip and see for my new puppy?

245
00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.200
Can I do that?

246
00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:05.840
Yes.

247
00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:06.720
Okay, good.

248
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:08.700
That's all I got, Ange.

249
00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:09.620
Anything you want to add?

250
00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.040
Do you have a Kinsey tidbit today?

251
00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.780
Well, here's a little Kinsey tidbit.

252
00:10:12.980 --> 00:10:17.920
One of the things Erin said, which we have talked about before, is how long these scripts came in.

253
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:22.680
You know, they were pretty big episodes, and then we would have to just chisel away,

254
00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:25.200
and there was so much that didn't make it to the screen.

255
00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.280
For example, this episode came in at 49 pages, right?

256
00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:30.220
Wait, what?

257
00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:33.700
The script for this was 49 pages?

