00:43:43.800 --> 00:43:44.180
Yes.

990
00:43:44.420 --> 00:43:49.600
We had a fan question from Beth E., Rachel R., Amy F., and Julie G.

991
00:43:49.820 --> 00:43:55.060
How many of those strollers did you use for the scenes where Dwight tests its durability?

992
00:43:55.740 --> 00:43:59.280
This is why I was on the phone with Phil Schafer an hour yesterday.

993
00:44:00.240 --> 00:44:02.540
Well, how many did they use?

994
00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:03.920
I'll tell you.

995
00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:10.100
It started with Phil Schafer doing a ton of research to find the perfect stroller for this

996
00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:10.580
episode.

997
00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:18.140
He said he and Greg had a long talk about how Jan was the kind of person who would have to

998
00:44:18.140 --> 00:44:19.460
have the fanciest stroller.

999
00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:26.740
In the script, it's written that the stroller is indestructible, and he wanted to find something

1000
00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:29.440
that would even look out of place in Scranton.

1001
00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:36.860
So he went on a search for an indestructible luxury stroller, and he found out about the

1002
00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:41.180
Orbit baby stroller, which was kind of a startup stroller at the time.

1003
00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:47.280
It launched in September of 2006, so just two years before this episode.

1004
00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:51.400
It was invented by two dads, Brian White and Joseph High.

1005
00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:52.660
I think it's High.

1006
00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:54.600
Joseph, I'm sorry if I got that wrong.

1007
00:44:54.840 --> 00:45:01.400
And they made it because Brian had just become a dad, and he said that his back really hurt

1008
00:45:01.400 --> 00:45:06.620
whenever he was trying to get the baby in and out of the car seat in the car.

1009
00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:11.100
So in his garage, they made a wooden prototype.

1010
00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:16.740
They studied all kinds of things, but they also sought to use green materials.

1011
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:17.880
I like these fellas.

